 {d:'B',s:'A',q:'Why should you drink water when you exercise?',o:['To replace the water lost in sweat','To make your muscles heavier','To stop your heart beating'],why:'The body loses water as sweat, so we need to drink to replace it.'},
 {d:'B',s:'K',q:'Which scientist wrote a famous book explaining evolution by natural selection?',o:['Charles Darwin','Isaac Newton','Alessandro Volta'],why:'Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859.'},
 {d:'B',s:'K',q:'Alfred Russel Wallace is famous for…',o:['also developing the theory of evolution by natural selection','inventing the battery','discovering the planets'],why:'Wallace came up with similar ideas to Darwin at the same time.'},
 {d:'B',s:'K',q:'Fossils show that some animals that lived long ago, such as dinosaurs, are now…',o:['extinct','living in the sea','very common'],why:'Extinct means there are none of that kind of animal left alive.'},
 {d:'B',s:'K',q:'Characteristics that are passed from parents to their offspring are called…',o:['inherited characteristics','adaptations to the weather','learned skills'],why:'Inherited characteristics, like natural eye colour, come from parents.'},
 {d:'B',s:'A',q:'A cactus lives in a hot desert. How is it adapted?',o:['It stores water in its stem and has spines instead of leaves','It has large, thin leaves','It grows only in ponds'],why:'Storing water and having spines help the cactus survive with very little rain.'},
 {d:'B',s:'A',q:'An owl hunts at night. Why does it have big eyes and soft feathers?',o:['To see in low light and fly silently','To swim underwater','To keep cool in the desert'],why:'These adaptations help owls catch prey in the dark.'},
 {d:'B',s:'A',q:'An Arctic hare has white fur in winter and brown fur in summer. How does this help it survive?',o:['It is camouflaged all year round','It keeps the hare cool in winter','It helps the hare swim'],why:'Matching its surroundings helps the hare hide from predators.'},
 {d:'B',s:'A',q:'An environment changes quickly. What might happen to a species that is not adapted to the change?',o:['It may become extinct','It will always grow bigger','Nothing will happen'],why:'Living things that are not suited to their environment may not survive.'},
 {d:'B',s:'W',tb:[['Beak shape','Food it is best suited to'],['Short and thick','Hard seeds'],['Long and thin','Insects in tree bark'],['Hooked','Meat']],q:'A bird has a long, thin beak. What is it most likely to eat?',o:['Insects from tree bark','Hard seeds','Meat'],why:'The table shows that long, thin beaks suit insects hiding in bark.'},
 {d:'B',s:'A',t:'two',q:'Which TWO features help a fish to live in water?',o:['Gills','Fins','Fur','Lungs'],why:'Gills take oxygen from water and fins help fish swim.'},
 {d:'B',s:'K',q:'What is variation?',o:['The differences between living things of the same kind','A type of fossil','When an animal changes colour'],why:'Offspring vary and are not identical to their parents or to each other.'},
 {d:'C',s:'K',q:'Which change is reversible?',o:['Melting chocolate','Burning paper','Baking bread'],why:'Melted chocolate can be cooled to become solid again.'},
 {d:'C',s:'A',q:'How could you separate a mixture of salt and sand?',o:['Add water, filter out the sand, then evaporate the water','Use a magnet','Use a sieve with large holes'],why:'Salt dissolves but sand does not. Filtering removes the sand, and evaporating leaves the salt.'},
 {d:'C',s:'K',q:'Which material is a good thermal insulator?',o:['Wool','Copper','Aluminium'],why:'Wool traps air, which slows the movement of heat.'},
 {d:'C',s:'K',q:'Which of these is soluble in water?',o:['Sugar','Sand','Chalk'],why:'Sugar dissolves in water. Sand and chalk do not.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',v:'holes:line',q:'Can the eye see the torch light through the holes in the cards?',o:['Yes, because the holes are in a straight line','No, light cannot pass through holes','Only if the torch is switched off'],why:'Light travels in straight lines, so it passes straight through the lined-up holes.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',v:'holes:offset',q:'One card has moved. Can the eye see the light now?',o:['No, because light cannot bend around the card','Yes, light curves around the card','Yes, but it is brighter'],why:'Light travels in straight lines, so the moved card blocks it.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',q:'In a ray diagram showing how we see a book, which way should the arrows point?',o:['From the light source to the book, then from the book to the eye','From the eye to the book','From the book to the light source'],why:'Light travels from the source, reflects off the book and enters the eye.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',q:'Which of these is a light source?',o:['A lit candle','A mirror','The Moon'],why:'A light source gives out its own light. Mirrors and the Moon only reflect light.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',q:'Why do cyclists wear reflective strips at night?',o:['They reflect car headlights, so drivers can see them','They make their own light','They keep the cyclist warm'],why:'Reflective materials send light back towards its source, such as a car’s headlights.'},
 {d:'P',s:'W',tb:[['Material','Light meter reading through the material'],['No material','100'],['Clear plastic','95'],['Tracing paper','60'],['Card','0']],q:'Which material is opaque?',o:['Card','Tracing paper','Clear plastic'],why:'No light passed through the card, so it is opaque.'},
 {d:'P',s:'W',tb:[['Material','Light meter reading through the material'],['No material','100'],['Clear plastic','95'],['Tracing paper','60'],['Card','0']],q:'Which material is translucent?',o:['Tracing paper','Card','No material'],why:'Some light passed through the tracing paper, but not all. It is translucent.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',q:'When are shadows outside at their longest?',o:['Early in the morning or late in the evening','At midday','They are always the same length'],why:'When the Sun is low in the sky, shadows are long.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',q:'Where does a shadow form?',o:['On the opposite side of the object from the light source','On the same side as the light source','Inside the object'],why:'The object blocks the light, so the shadow is behind it.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',v:'symbol:motor',q:'What does this circuit symbol represent?',o:['A motor','A lamp','A cell'],why:'A circle with the letter M is the symbol for a motor.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',v:'symbol:buzzer',q:'What does this circuit symbol represent?',o:['A buzzer','A switch','A motor'],why:'This is the symbol for a buzzer.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',v:'symbol:switchclosed',q:'What does this circuit symbol represent?',o:['A closed switch','An open switch','A cell'],why:'There is no gap, so the switch is closed and electricity can flow.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',v:'symcircuit:twobulb',q:'Two lamps are in series with one cell. Compared with one lamp on its own, how bright is each lamp?',o:['Dimmer','Brighter','Exactly the same'],why:'The two lamps share the voltage, so each is dimmer.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',v:'symcircuit:buzzer',q:'Look at the circuit diagram. Will the buzzer sound?',o:['Yes, it is a complete circuit','No, the switch is open','No, there is no cell'],why:'The circuit is complete, so electricity flows through the buzzer.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',q:'Which change would make a motor spin faster?',o:['Adding another cell','Adding another motor in series','Removing a cell'],why:'More cells give a higher voltage, so the motor spins faster.'},
 {d:'P',s:'A',q:'A circuit has two cells and one lamp. One cell is removed. What happens to the lamp?',o:['It gets dimmer','It gets brighter','It stays the same'],why:'Lower voltage means the lamp is dimmer.'},
 {d:'P',s:'W',tb:[['Number of cells','Motor speed (turns per minute)'],['1','60'],['2','120'],['3','180']],q:'Use the pattern. Predict the motor speed with 4 cells.',o:['About 240 turns per minute','About 60 turns per minute','About 180 turns per minute'],why:'Each extra cell adds about 60 turns per minute.'},
 {d:'P',s:'W',tb:[['Number of cells','Motor speed (turns per minute)'],['1','60'],['2','120'],['3','180']],q:'Which variable was changed in this investigation?',o:['The number of cells','The motor speed','The type of motor'],why:'The number of cells was changed, and the motor speed was measured.'},
 {d:'P',s:'K',q:'Why do scientists draw circuit diagrams using symbols?',o:['Symbols are quick to draw and understood everywhere','Symbols look more colourful','Symbols make circuits work better'],why:'Recognised symbols mean anyone can read a circuit diagram.'},
 {d:'P',s:'W',q:'Why is it important to repeat measurements in an investigation?',o:['To check the results are reliable','To make the investigation longer','To change the variable'],why:'Repeat readings help you spot mistakes and make results more reliable.'}
